NAME
       gpasswd - manage the members and administrators of a group

SYNOPSIS
       gpasswd -a USER GROUP
       gpasswd -d USER GROUP
       gpasswd -M USER[,USER...] GROUP
       gpasswd -A USER[,USER...] GROUP
       gpasswd [-r | -R] GROUP

DESCRIPTION
       gpasswd changes /etc/group and /etc/gshadow. Its most common use is
       adding one user to a group or removing one, without touching the
       user's other groups:

           gpasswd -a student devops
           Adding user student to group devops

       root may do anything. A group may also have administrators (set with
       -A), who may add and remove members of that group themselves.

       Group membership is read at login: a user who is logged in sees a new
       group only after logging in again (or in a new shell started with
       newgrp or su - USER).

       With only a GROUP name, gpasswd asks for a group password. Group
       passwords are rarely used.

OPTIONS
       -a USER, --add USER
              Add USER to GROUP.

       -d USER, --delete USER
              Remove USER from GROUP.

       -M LIST, --members LIST
              Set the whole member list (replaces the old members).

       -A LIST, --administrators LIST
              Set the group's administrators.

       -r     Remove the group password.

       -R     Restrict access: only members can use newgrp to join it.

EXAMPLES
       Add student to devops and check:

           gpasswd -a student devops
           id student

       Take a user out of wheel (no more sudo through wheel):

           gpasswd -d alice wheel

       Make the group's member list exactly two users:

           gpasswd -M alice,bob devops

NOTES
       usermod -aG GROUP USER also adds a user to a group. Without -a,
       usermod -G replaces all of the user's supplementary groups, which is a
       common mistake; gpasswd -a cannot make it.

FILES
       /etc/group, /etc/gshadow

SEE ALSO
       groupadd(8), groupmod(8), usermod(8), newgrp(1), id(1), group(5)
